---
import ModuleLayout from '../../layouts/ModuleLayout.astro';
import modules from '../../data/modules.json';
import '../../styles/modules/sports.css';
import '../../styles/icons/sports.css';

const mod = modules.find((m) => m.id === 41)!;
---

<ModuleLayout mod={mod}>

  

    <div class="module-header">
      <i class="fa-solid fa-person-skiing module-icon-large"></i>
      <h1 class="module-title module-heading">Module 41: Sports</h1>
    </div>
    <p class="module-subtitle">Why sport matters, how it works, and a deep dive into the world's game</p>

    <!-- ── PART A ── -->
    <h2 class="sec" id="part-a-what-sport-is-competition-culture-and-the-human-body">Part A · what sport is: competition, culture, and the human body</h2>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="clabel">Overview</div>
      <p class="overview-lede">
        Sport is one of the oldest and most universal human activities, yet it is also surprisingly hard to define. At its core, sport combines physical skill, rule-bound competition, and a social audience, distinguishing it from mere play (no rules, no audience) and from games like chess (no physical skill threshold). This module builds a complete mental model of sport: why humans play, how bodies and minds perform, who controls the money, and how the world's most popular game, association football, works at every level from kick-off to contract clause.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Why humans play -->
    <div class="card">
      <div class="clabel">Why humans play sport</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Sport solves several problems at once. Evolutionarily, it is honest signalling: costly physical performance that cannot easily be faked, advertising genetic quality, coordination ability, and pain tolerance to potential mates and allies. Johan Huizinga's 1938 concept of "Homo Ludens" argues that play is not a byproduct of survival but constitutive of culture: that meaningful human civilization requires it. Sport also generates <em>flow states</em>: Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi's term for the absorption that occurs when challenge precisely matches skill. The brain releases dopamine, noradrenaline, and anandamide during intense play, making winning biologically rewarding.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Sociologically, sport creates <em>in-group cohesion</em> and shared identity. Henri Tajfel's social identity theory explains why fans experience their team's win as a personal victory: group membership is internalized as self-concept. Roughly 70% of British men report that following sport is important to their sense of identity (YouGov, 2019). This attachment is not irrational; it generates genuine community, ritual, and belonging that modern life often fails to provide elsewhere.
      </p>
      <div class="info-box">
        Counterintuitive fact: sport watching produces measurable testosterone spikes in fans of the winning team, even when they did nothing physical. A 1998 study found male Brazil fans' testosterone rose ~28% after their team won a World Cup match, and dropped a similar amount after a loss. Identity and biology are inseparable here.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Sport vs game vs play -->
    <div class="card">
      <div class="clabel">Sport vs games vs play: where the lines fall</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        The philosopher Bernard Suits defined sport as an activity requiring physical skill, rules, and an institutional structure (governing body, ranked competition). By this definition, chess is a game but not a sport; jogging is physical recreation but not sport; poker is a game. The boundary matters practically: the IOC debates whether eSports and chess deserve Olympic status on exactly these grounds.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph-last">
        A key property of sport is the <em>constitutive rule</em>: rules that don't just regulate an activity but create it. The offside rule doesn't restrict football; it creates football as we know it. This is why sports feel so different from work: the voluntary acceptance of unnecessary constraints is what makes the achievement meaningful. Usain Bolt running 100 metres is meaningless unless everyone agrees to start at the same line and not take a bus.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- History of organised sport -->
    <div class="card">
      <div class="clabel">The history of organised sport</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        The ancient Olympics began in 776 BCE at Olympia, Greece, as a religious festival honouring Zeus. Events included the stadion foot-race (roughly 192 metres), wrestling, discus, javelin, and chariot racing. Competitors were Greek citizens who trained full-time, often sponsored by wealthy patrons, not amateur idealists. The games were abolished by Emperor Theodosius I in 393 CE as pagan practice.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Modern codified sport emerged in 19th-century Britain, where industrialisation compressed work and leisure into distinct spheres, and railway networks allowed teams to travel. The Football Association was founded in 1863. The Marquess of Queensberry Rules for boxing appeared in 1867. Wimbledon launched in 1877. This codification wave was also a class project: elite schools (Eton, Rugby) formalized games that had previously been unruly mob activities.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph-last">
        Television transformed sport into a global product. The 1936 Berlin Olympics were the first televised games (to 28 closed-circuit venues). By 1996, Atlanta attracted 3.5 billion TV viewers. Today, the Super Bowl generates ~$600 million in US ad revenue in a single evening, and the FIFA World Cup final is reliably the most watched single broadcast event on Earth, drawing over 1.5 billion viewers in 2022.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Most popular sports — bar chart -->
    <div class="card">
      <div class="clabel">The world's most popular sports: estimated global following</div>
      <div class="sport-pop-chart" id="sport-pop-chart"></div>
      <p class="chart-note">Estimates of "global following" combine active participation and regular viewership. Sources: World Atlas, Statista 2023. Cricket's following is heavily concentrated in South Asia; football and basketball have broader geographic spread.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- ── PART B ── -->
    <h2 class="sec" id="part-b-the-science-of-sport-physiology-psychology-and-performance">Part B · the science of sport: physiology, psychology, and performance</h2>

    <!-- Human limits + comparison scale -->
    <div class="card">
      <div class="clabel">Human physical limits: what the body can and cannot do</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph-spaced">
        VO₂ max, the maximum rate of oxygen consumption, is the single best predictor of endurance performance. Average untrained adults score 35–45 ml/kg/min. Elite cyclists like Oskar Syklebust have recorded values above 97. The practical ceiling may be around 100, dictated by cardiac output and the diffusion capacity of the lungs. Eliud Kipchoge, who ran the first sub-2-hour marathon in 2019, has a VO₂ max of ~92.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph-spaced">
        Muscle fibres determine athletic specialisation. Type I (slow-twitch) fibres use oxygen efficiently and resist fatigue: marathoners have roughly 80% Type I. Type II (fast-twitch) fibres contract explosively but tire quickly: sprinters may have 75% Type II. This ratio is ~50% genetic, making a true sprinter becoming a world-class marathon runner essentially impossible at elite level.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph-spaced">
        The 100-metre sprint ceiling: biomechanical models suggest the absolute human limit is approximately 9.48 seconds, versus Usain Bolt's 9.58 in 2009. The constraint is not just muscle speed but ground contact time: below about 80 milliseconds per step, the foot cannot generate enough force.
      </p>
      <div class="clabel u-mt-md">Physical demand by sport (illustrative comparison)</div>
      <div class="block-margin">
        <div class="cscale-poles"><span>Pure endurance</span><span>Pure power / speed</span></div>
        <div class="data-row-wide"><div class="cscale-name">Marathon</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-data" style="--data-left: 5%"></div></div></div>
        <div class="data-row-wide"><div class="cscale-name">Tour de France cyclist</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-data" style="--data-left: 12%"></div></div></div>
        <div class="data-row-wide"><div class="cscale-name">Football (midfield)</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-data" style="--data-left: 40%"></div></div></div>
        <div class="data-row-wide"><div class="cscale-name">NBA basketball</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-data" style="--data-left: 55%"></div></div></div>
        <div class="data-row-wide"><div class="cscale-name">200m swimmer</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-data" style="--data-left: 65%"></div></div></div>
        <div class="data-row-wide"><div class="cscale-name">100m sprint</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-data" style="--data-left: 93%"></div></div></div>
        <div class="data-row-wide"><div class="cscale-name">Powerlifting</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-data" style="--data-left: 98%"></div></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Training principles + VO2 slider -->
    <div class="card">
      <div class="clabel">Training principles: how elite athletes improve</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        <em>Progressive overload</em> is the foundational principle: the training stimulus must continually exceed the body's current capacity to produce adaptation. Rest is not recovery from failure; it is when the adaptation actually occurs (supercompensation). This is why overtraining is a real pathology: athletes who never allow recovery plateau or regress.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        <em>Periodisation</em> structures training across cycles: a macrocycle (full season or year), mesocycles (4–6 weeks each with different emphases), and microcycles (weekly training blocks). The Soviet sports scientist Lev Matveyev formalized this in the 1960s, and it remains the backbone of elite preparation. Modern variants include "block periodisation" used by Norwegian swimmer Henrik Christiansen: intense focus on one physical quality at a time rather than concurrent development.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph-spaced">
        The "10,000 hours" rule from Malcolm Gladwell (drawn from Anders Ericsson's research) is widely misunderstood. Ericsson's actual finding was that deliberate practice, not passive repetition, drives expertise, and the 10,000 figure was an average, not a threshold. Some elite tennis players (Roger Federer, who had a late start in tennis specifically) accumulated far fewer hours. Genetics, coaching quality, and early specialisation age all interact with practice volume.
      </p>
      <div class="clabel">VO₂ max improvement simulator</div>
      <div class="slider-wrap">
        <div class="pole-labels-top"><span>0 weeks of training</span><span>52 weeks</span></div>
        <input type="range" id="training-weeks" min="0" max="52" value="12" step="1">
        <div class="slider-out" id="training-out">Move the slider to see estimated VO₂ max improvement.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Sports psychology -->
    <div class="card">
      <div class="clabel">Sports psychology: concentration, pressure, and the mental game</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        <em>Choking</em> occurs when performance degrades under pressure despite sufficient skill. The mechanism is well understood: anxiety redirects conscious attention toward the execution of actions normally automated. Jana Sackmeister and colleagues demonstrated in 2010 that expert golfers putt worse when told to focus on "keeping the left arm straight": a cue novices benefit from but experts don't, because their swing is already implicit. This is why commentary about "thinking too much" is neurologically accurate.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        <em>Flow state</em>, Csikszentmihalyi's concept of total absorption, correlates with a specific EEG pattern: increased alpha waves in the left temporal lobe, associated with reduced self-monitoring. Athletes describe it as effortless control, slowed time, and the disappearance of self-consciousness. Conditions for flow: clear goals, immediate feedback, and challenge-skill balance within roughly 4% of current capacity.
      </p>
      <div class="warn-box">
        Common misconception: positive self-talk ("I can do this") is always better than negative self-talk. Research by Antonis Hatzigeorgiadis (2011) found instructional self-talk ("bend your knees on contact") outperforms motivational self-talk for skill-based tasks. The right mental cue depends entirely on what the task requires.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Doping -->
    <div class="card">
      <div class="clabel">Nutrition and doping: optimisation vs cheating</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Sports nutrition operates on marginal gains. Creatine monohydrate is the most evidence-supported legal supplement: 3–5g daily increases phosphocreatine stores in muscle, improving high-intensity performance by 5–10% in trained athletes. Caffeine (3–6 mg/kg body weight) demonstrably reduces perceived exertion and improves endurance by 2–4%, which at the elite level is the difference between a medal and 10th place. Both are legal.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Performance-enhancing drug use has accompanied modern sport since the 1950s. EPO (erythropoietin) revolutionised cycling in the 1990s: it stimulates red blood cell production, raising haematocrit and VO₂ max by up to 10%. The USADA found that Lance Armstrong's US Postal Service team ran "the most sophisticated, professionalised, and successful doping programme" in sport history; Armstrong won 7 consecutive Tours de France between 1999–2005, all of which were later stripped.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph-last">
        WADA (World Anti-Doping Agency), established in 1999, maintains the Prohibited List of ~300 substances across categories. The biological passport, introduced in 2008, tracks longitudinal blood parameters rather than testing for specific substances, making it much harder to dope without detection. Russia's state-sponsored doping programme (revealed by whistleblower Grigory Rodchenkov, 2015) involved switching urine samples through a hole in a Sochi lab wall, rendering standard testing useless.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- ── PART C ── -->
    <h2 class="sec" id="part-c-the-business-and-politics-of-sport">Part C · the business and politics of sport</h2>

    <!-- Sports economy -->
    <div class="card">
      <div class="clabel">The sports economy: how sport makes and spends money</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Global sport is a $500+ billion industry. The dominant revenue engine is broadcast rights, not ticket sales or merchandise. The NFL's TV rights deals (2023–2033) total $113 billion across CBS, NBC, Fox, ESPN/ABC, and Amazon, approximately $11 billion per year for one domestic league. The Premier League earns ~£3 billion per season from UK broadcast deals alone, plus ~£1.6 billion from international rights.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Athlete salaries at the top tier are extraordinary. As of 2024, Cristiano Ronaldo earns approximately $200 million per year total (salary + commercial), making him the world's highest-paid athlete. The NBA minimum rookie salary is $1.1 million; the median is $3.8 million. But these figures obscure the pyramid structure: the vast majority of professional athletes globally earn near minimum wage, and career lengths in contact sports average just 3–4 years.
      </p>
      <div class="clabel u-mt-md">Athlete salary comparison (annual, USD, approximate 2024)</div>
      <div id="salary-chart"></div>
      <p class="chart-note">Total compensation including endorsements. Sources: Forbes, Sportico 2024.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Governance -->
    <div class="card">
      <div class="clabel">Sports governance, who runs sport and the corruption problem</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Most global sports are governed by non-profit bodies with enormous commercial revenues and minimal external accountability. FIFA, which governs football globally, had $7.5 billion revenue in the 2022 World Cup cycle. The IOC controls a multi-billion-dollar broadcast empire while its 105 members are unelected and permanent. This structural opacity has generated systemic corruption.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        The 2015 FBI/DoJ investigation into FIFA indicted 47 individuals and entities for wire fraud, racketeering, and money laundering totalling $200 million in bribes. Sepp Blatter and Michel Platini were banned for 8 years. The Salt Lake City Olympics bribery scandal (1998) involved 13 IOC members accepting money or gifts in exchange for hosting votes.
      </p>
      <div class="info-box">
        The structural problem: international sports federations are often registered in Switzerland, which has historically provided legal shelter from foreign law enforcement. FIFA is incorporated in Zurich. Post-2015, the US relied on wire fraud statutes, asserting jurisdiction whenever a US dollar crossed a US server, to prosecute what Swiss law could not reach.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Sport and politics -->
    <div class="card">
      <div class="clabel">Sport and politics: when they cannot be separated</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        The 1936 Berlin Olympics were explicitly designed by the Nazi regime as a propaganda showcase. Jesse Owens' four gold medals, before an audience that included Hitler, are remembered as a rebuke to Aryan superiority ideology. In fact, Owens himself said Hitler waved to him from his box; it was President Roosevelt who never sent Owens a telegram of congratulation. The politics cut in multiple directions.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Apartheid-era South Africa was expelled from the IOC in 1970 and FIFA in 1961. The international sporting boycott is widely cited as one of the most effective non-military economic and reputational pressures applied to the regime, contributing to its eventual isolation. Colin Kaepernick's NFL kneeling protests (2016–) cost him his career but generated a global conversation: Nike's "Believe in Something" campaign featuring Kaepernick reportedly increased Nike's online sales 31% in the week after launch.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph-last">
        <em>Sportswashing</em>, using sport to launder a country's international reputation, is the dominant political concern of the 2020s. Saudi Arabia's Public Investment Fund owns Newcastle United, LIV Golf, the Dakar Rally, and has hosted heavyweight boxing world title fights. Qatar hosted the 2022 World Cup despite evidence of migrant worker deaths during construction. The argument that "engagement beats isolation" has merit but also serves as a post-hoc justification for financial self-interest by governing bodies.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Analytics -->
    <div class="card">
      <div class="clabel">Analytics and data: how sport became a data science problem</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        The Moneyball revolution, documented by Michael Lewis in 2003, describes how the Oakland Athletics under GM Billy Beane used on-base percentage (OBP), a statistic ignored by the market, to acquire undervalued players and compete with a payroll 40% below the Yankees. The underlying insight was that human scouts systematically misprice players because they weight observable, emotionally salient qualities (appearance, confidence, throwing style) over predictive statistics. The A's won 103 games in 2002 with a $44 million payroll.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Football's data revolution came later. Opta began collecting event data in 1996; StatsBomb launched player tracking in 2014. Expected goals (xG) (the probability a given shot results in a goal, based on location, assist type, and body part) is now the standard metric. Clubs like Brentford and Brighton, with modest budgets, built analytical departments that identified undervalued players: Brighton signed Alexis Mac Allister (later sold to Liverpool for £35m) for £7.2m using data-driven scouting.
      </p>
      <div class="warn-box">
        The limits of analytics: Goodhart's Law applies in sport. Once a metric becomes a target, it ceases to be a good metric. Teams optimised for xG began conceding counter-attacks that didn't show up in expected-goals models. The next frontier is tracking data: 25 position measurements per second per player, which captures shape, movement, and space rather than just ball events.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ════════════════════════════════════
         PARTS D–J: THE FOOTBALL DEEP DIVE
    ════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <div class="sec-football">⚽ Football deep dive: Parts D through J</div>
    <div class="football-banner">
      Football (association football / soccer) is the world's most played and watched sport, with ~3.5 billion followers across every continent. Parts D–J are dedicated entirely to understanding it at depth: history, tactics, structure, economics, and culture.
    </div>

    <!-- ── PART D ── -->
    <div class="sec-football">Part D · football basics: rules, positions, and the structure of play</div>

    <!-- Laws of the game -->
    <div class="card-football card">
      <div class="clabel">The Laws of the Game</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Football has exactly 17 Laws, maintained by the International Football Association Board (IFAB), founded in 1886. The laws are elegant in their brevity: the entire document is shorter than an average mortgage agreement. But three laws generate the most controversy in practice.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        <em>Offside (Law 11)</em> is the most misunderstood. A player is in an offside position if any part of their body that can legally play the ball is nearer to the opponents' goal line than both the ball and the second-to-last defender at the moment the ball is played. The key word is "played", not when the player receives the ball. This is why VAR sometimes checks whether an armpit or kneecap was forward by 2 centimetres when a pass was struck.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        <em>VAR</em> was mandated in 2018 World Cup and has since spread to most top leagues. It can only review four categories: goals, penalties, red cards, and mistaken identity. The "clear and obvious error" threshold was designed to preserve match flow, but in practice many leagues have dropped this threshold, creating lengthy delays. Fan satisfaction with VAR is net negative in most surveys.
      </p>
      <div class="info-box">
        The handball rule has been changed five times since 2016 and remains contested. The current law distinguishes between deliberate handball (always a foul), handball that directly leads to a goal (foul even if accidental), and incidental handball during natural arm position (not a foul). The "natural silhouette" test is applied by referees, but no two referees draw this silhouette identically.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Positions diagram -->
    <div class="card-football card">
      <div class="clabel">Positions and their roles: a visual overview</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph-spaced">
        A standard 4-3-3 formation places players in three banks. Each position has a primary responsibility and a pressing responsibility when the team is without the ball. The diagram below shows a 4-3-3 with approximate pitch zones.
      </p>
      <div class="diagram-wrap">
        <svg class="sports-media" viewBox="0 0 520 340" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
          <!-- Pitch -->
          <rect x="20" y="10" width="480" height="320" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="var(--color-border-secondary)" stroke-width="1.2"/>
          <!-- Halfway line -->
          <line x1="20" y1="170" x2="500" y2="170" stroke="var(--color-border-tertiary)" stroke-width="0.8" stroke-dasharray="6,4"/>
