          <text x="390" y="174" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" class="pos-label-team" font-weight="700">ST</text>
          <circle cx="360" cy="220" r="13" fill="#DC2626" opacity="0.85"/>
          <text x="360" y="224" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" class="pos-label-team" font-weight="700">RW</text>
          <!-- Direction arrow -->
          <defs><marker id="arr" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="5" refY="3.5" orient="auto"><polygon points="0 0, 7 3.5, 0 7" fill="var(--color-text-tertiary)"/></marker></defs>
          <line x1="430" y1="315" x2="480" y2="315" stroke="var(--color-text-tertiary)" stroke-width="1" marker-end="url(#arr)"/>
          <text x="426" y="310" font-size="9" fill="var(--color-text-tertiary)" text-anchor="end">Attack</text>
          <!-- Legend -->
          <circle cx="30" cy="25" r="7" fill="#5E728C"/>
          <text x="40" y="29" font-size="9" fill="var(--color-text-secondary)">Defenders/GK</text>
          <circle cx="130" cy="25" r="7" fill="#2563EB"/>
          <text x="140" y="29" font-size="9" fill="var(--color-text-secondary)">Midfielders</text>
          <circle cx="220" cy="25" r="7" fill="#DC2626"/>
          <text x="230" y="29" font-size="9" fill="var(--color-text-secondary)">Forwards</text>
        </svg>
      </div>
      <p class="body-paragraph-after">
        The <em>false 9</em> is a striker who drops into midfield rather than staying central, dragging centre-backs out of position. Messi played the false 9 for Guardiola's Barcelona from 2008, creating space for the wide forwards (Pedro, Villa) to attack the now-unoccupied centre. Wing-backs, in a back-three system, have a dual role as wide defenders and attacking outlets, covering more ground (10–13km per match) than almost any other position.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Phases of play -->
    <div class="card-football card">
      <div class="clabel">The structure of a match: phases of play</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Modern tactical analysis divides a match into four phases: in possession, out of possession, transition to attack (winning the ball), and transition to defence (losing the ball). The transitions are often the most decisive moments: teams are most vulnerable in the 5 seconds after winning or losing the ball, when organisation breaks down.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        A <em>pressing trigger</em> is a moment that signals the team should press collectively. Common triggers: the opposition goalkeeper has the ball (limited options), a heavy touch by an opposition player, a backward pass by a defender. Klopp's Liverpool (2016–2024) pressed at 82.8 PPDA (passes per defensive action) in their peak seasons, meaning they allowed opponents fewer than 1 pass per press attempt on average.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph-last">
        The <em>high block</em> (pressing high up the pitch) and <em>low block</em> (defending deep) represent opposite philosophies with different risk profiles. A high block generates turnovers near the opponent's goal (high reward if successful) but concedes large spaces behind the defensive line (high risk if beaten). Teams with inferior athletes often prefer low blocks, like José Mourinho's Inter in the 2010 Champions League final, which contained an exceptional Barcelona squad with a disciplined 4-5-1 and won 2–0.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- ── PART E ── -->
    <div class="sec-football">Part E · football tactics: formations, systems, and philosophy</div>

    <!-- Tactics timeline -->
    <div class="card-football card">
      <div class="clabel">The history of football tactics: timeline</div>
      <div id="tactics-timeline" class="block-margin"></div>
      <p class="chart-note">Era labels are approximate. Tactical eras overlap significantly: the 4-4-2 remained common in England until the 2010s while continental football had moved on.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Formation explorer -->
    <div class="card-football card">
      <div class="clabel">Major formations: click to explore</div>
      <div class="explorer-btns" id="formation-btns"></div>
      <div class="explorer-panel" id="formation-panel">
        <div class="ep-title">Select a formation above</div>
        <div class="ep-body">Click any button to see how that formation works, its strengths, weaknesses, and which managers made it famous.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Pressing -->
    <div class="card-football card">
      <div class="clabel">Pressing: the tactical revolution of the 2010s</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Gegenpressing ("counter-pressing" in German) refers to immediately winning the ball back after losing possession, before the opposition can reorganize. Jürgen Klopp developed it at Borussia Dortmund between 2008–2015, drawing on Ralf Rangnick's predecessor work at RB Leipzig. The logic: for 5–8 seconds after losing the ball, attacking players are closer to the ball than defenders, creating a brief window to win it back in a dangerous area.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        <em>PPDA</em> (Passes Allowed Per Defensive Action) is the standard press-intensity metric. Lower PPDA = more aggressive pressing. Elite pressing teams like Klopp's Liverpool 2019–20 averaged ~6 PPDA; a passive team averages 12–18. The physical cost is significant: high-press teams cover 3–4 km more than opponents in a match and see injury rates correlate with press intensity across a season.
      </p>
      <div class="warn-box">
        The press has limits. Low-quality opposition can be pressed off the pitch; elite technical players (Iniesta, De Bruyne, Modric) can escape presses with first-touch control and short combinations. Manchester City under Guardiola specifically trains "press-breaking" patterns as a primary possession objective.
      </div>

      <div class="clabel gap-above-lg">Press intensity comparison: PPDA (lower = more aggressive press)</div>
      <div class="u-mt-sm" id="ppda-chart"></div>
      <p class="data-note">PPDA figures are approximate seasonal averages. Sources: StatsBomb, Understat 2019–23.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Positional football -->
    <div class="card-football card">
      <div class="clabel">Build-up play and positional football: Guardiola and the Spanish school</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        <em>Juego de posición</em> (positional play) was systematized by Johan Cruyff at Barcelona from 1988 and codified by Pep Guardiola, who played under Cruyff before managing the club. Its core principle: always maintain numerical and positional superiority in every zone of the pitch. The objective of ball possession is not possession itself (as critics claim) but to create asymmetric spaces the opponent cannot cover.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        The <em>half-space</em>, the zone between the centre and the wide channel, is the critical area in positional play. It is the hardest zone for a back-four to defend because it is diagonal from both centre-backs and full-backs. Guardiola's teams specifically position inverted wingers and midfielders in half-spaces to receive, turn, and threaten goal.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph-last">
        An <em>inverted winger</em> is a right-footed player who plays on the left wing (or vice versa), so they cut inside onto their stronger foot rather than crossing. Arjen Robben at Bayern Munich and Franck Ribéry provided a template: Robben's cut-inside-and-shoot pattern was so well-known that opposing full-backs could do nothing to stop it: the sequence was so fast and precise that predictability didn't diminish effectiveness.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Set pieces -->
    <div class="card-football card">
      <div class="clabel">Set pieces: the neglected winning margin</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Approximately 30% of all goals at the 2022 World Cup came directly or indirectly from set pieces (corners, free kicks, and throw-ins). This figure has risen steadily since 2010, driven by increasing investment in set-piece coaching. Yet as recently as 2014, most elite clubs had no dedicated set-piece coach. Denmark was the first international side to formally employ one (full-time, 2010s).
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Corner routines use coordinated movement patterns to create separation. The simplest mechanism: decoy runners drag markers away, while the true target arrives late into an uncontested zone. Liverpool's 2021–22 season included specifically designed "near-post flick" corners that relied on Trent Alexander-Arnold's delivery accuracy to within a 1-metre box.
      </p>
      <div class="info-box">
        Rory Delap's long throw-ins at Stoke City (2009–2012) created a specialised threat equivalent to a corner kick from open play. Delap could throw the ball 40+ metres, reaching the six-yard box. Opponents specifically prepared for Stoke's throw-ins in a way usually reserved for free kicks. Stoke finished 11th in the Premier League in 2009–10, comfortably beyond what their squad quality suggested was possible.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- xG calculator -->
    <div class="card-football card">
      <div class="clabel">Football analytics: xG, progressive passes, and the data revolution</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        <em>Expected goals (xG)</em> assigns each shot a probability between 0 and 1 that it results in a goal, based on historical data. A penalty is approximately 0.76 xG. A headed chance from 12 metres after a cross is ~0.09 xG. An open-play shot from the centre of the six-yard box is ~0.5 xG. Summing a team's xG over a season gives a better prediction of future performance than actual goals scored: teams who consistently outscore their xG tend to regress.
      </p>
      <div class="clabel u-mt-md">xG estimator: open-play shots</div>
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="calc-row">
          <div>
            <label for="xg-distance">Distance from goal (metres)</label>
            <input class="u-w-100pct" type="number" id="xg-distance" min="3" max="40" value="18">
          </div>
          <div>
            <label for="xg-angle">Shooting angle (degrees from centre, 0=straight-on, 45=wide)</label>
            <input class="u-w-100pct" type="number" id="xg-angle" min="0" max="70" value="10">
          </div>
          <div>
            <label for="xg-type">Shot type</label>
            <select class="xg-type" id="xg-type">
              <option value="foot">Foot shot</option>
              <option value="head">Header</option>
              <option value="volley">Volley</option>
            </select>
          </div>
          <div class="sports-div-style">
            <button onclick="calcXG()">Calculate xG</button>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="calc-out" id="xg-out">Enter shot details above and click Calculate.</div>
      </div>
      <p class="body-paragraph-last">
        <em>Progressive passes</em> move the ball at least 10 metres closer to the opponent's goal (StatsBomb definition). Teams with high progressive pass counts tend to have better positional control. The limitation of all current models: they measure ball events only. Context (whether defenders are tracking back, whether the goalkeeper is off his line) is largely absent.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- ── PART F ── -->
    <div class="sec-football">Part F · the history of football: from Sheffield to the global game</div>

    <!-- Invention of football -->
    <div class="card-football card">
      <div class="clabel">The invention of football: codification in 19th-century England</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        The founding myth of football assigns primacy to Cambridge and Sheffield, but the reality is messier. The 1848 Cambridge Rules were drawn up by students from different schools to enable inter-school matches; Sheffield FC, founded in 1857, developed its own code independently. The key divide was handling: rugby football allowed carrying the ball; association football banned it. The Football Association, formed in London on October 26, 1863, attempted to unify the various codes. Blackheath FC walked out when handling and hacking (kicking opponents) were prohibited: they eventually became part of the Rugby Football Union (founded 1871).
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph-last">
        Professionalism was a class battle. The FA banned paid players until 1885, partly because elite clubs feared northern working-class teams (whose players needed wages to compensate for lost work hours) would dominate. When Preston North End and other northern clubs threatened to break away and form a professional league, the FA relented. The Football League, the world's first professional football league, launched in 1888 with 12 founding clubs. Aston Villa won the first season.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Global spread -->
    <div class="card-football card">
      <div class="clabel">Football spreads globally: how British sailors and workers exported the game</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Football spread primarily through the informal networks of British commercial expansion: sailors, railway engineers, merchants, and teachers exported the game before any organised international effort. Genoa Cricket and Football Club (1893) is Italy's oldest surviving football club, founded by British expatriates. Club Atlético de Madrid was originally called Madrid Football Club when founded by students returning from England in 1903.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        South America developed its own football culture quickly. Uruguay won the first two Olympic football tournaments (1924, 1928) and hosted and won the inaugural World Cup in 1930. Brazil's hybrid style (shaped by African and European influences, formalized in the 1950s as <em>jogo bonito</em> (the beautiful game)) became the dominant aesthetic ideal globally after the 1970 World Cup.
      </p>
      <div class="info-box">
        Africa and Asia received football largely through British colonial administration. The Africa Cup of Nations began in 1957. Asian countries first competed in the Olympics in 1948. But the global football hierarchy, which still sees European clubs drain talent from South America and Africa, reflects colonial economic patterns: the best players from Lagos, Cairo, and Buenos Aires move to London, Barcelona, and Munich, not the reverse.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- World Cup history -->
    <div class="card-football card">
      <div class="clabel">The World Cup: winners and political context</div>
      <div id="wc-distbar">
        <p class="chart-intro">World Cup wins by nation (1930–2022, 22 tournaments)</p>
      </div>
      <p class="body-paragraph-after">
        The 1954 tournament is the "Miracle of Bern": West Germany beat Hungary 3–2 in the final after Hungary had demolished Germany 8–3 in the group stage. The 1970 Brazil team (Pelé, Jairzinho, Rivelino, Tostão) is considered by many analysts the greatest World Cup team ever; they are the only side to win every match in the tournament. The 1986 tournament belongs to Maradona: he scored both the "Hand of God" goal and the "Goal of the Century" against England in the same quarter-final, six minutes apart. Qatar 2022 was the first World Cup in the Middle East; the tournament generated $7.5 billion in FIFA revenue.
      </p>
    </div>

