      <div class="elem-detail" id="player-detail"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── PART G ── -->
    <div class="sec-football">Part G · the football industry: money, transfers, and power</div>

    <!-- Revenue streams -->
    <div class="card-football card">
      <div class="clabel">How football clubs make money: revenue streams and hierarchy</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph-spaced">
        The top 20 clubs by revenue (Deloitte Football Money League 2024) collectively earned €10.5 billion in 2022–23. Real Madrid topped the list at €831m, followed by Manchester City (€826m) and PSG (€804m). Revenue divides into three streams: broadcasting (largest, ~45%), commercial (sponsorship, shirt sales, ~35%), and matchday (tickets, hospitality, ~20%). The broadcasting share matters because it is the most volatile: the Premier League's next UK deal may be worth more or less than anticipated.
      </p>
      <div class="clabel">Revenue breakdown: typical top-10 Premier League club</div>
      <div id="revenue-distbar"></div>
      <p class="revenue-note">Approximate proportions vary by club size and stadium capacity. Source: Deloitte Football Money League 2024.</p>
      <div class="info-box">
        The revenue gap between the top and the rest is structural. Brighton, a well-run mid-table Premier League club, earned ~£181m in 2022–23, less than 22% of Real Madrid's income. This gap funds player quality: the difference in wage bills explains roughly 75% of league position variance across a season (Szymanski & Kuypers, 1999, still remarkably accurate in updated studies).
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Transfer market -->
    <div class="card-football card">
      <div class="clabel">The transfer market: how player buying and selling works</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Transfer fees are paid by the buying club to the selling club for a player's registration. The player themselves receives a signing-on fee and wage, negotiated separately. Agents, who represent players (and sometimes, controversially, clubs simultaneously), typically receive 3–10% of the fee and 10–15% of the player's wages. PSG paid €222 million to Barcelona for Neymar in 2017, still the world record. At that price, PSG paid approximately €11,000 per minute of football across his contract.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        <em>Sell-on clauses</em> entitle the selling club to a percentage of any future sale. Crystal Palace inserted a 15% sell-on clause when selling Wilfried Zaha to Manchester United for £15m in 2013; when Zaha eventually rejoined Palace and they sold him to Aston Villa for £30m in 2023, Palace received an additional share. Smaller clubs routinely use sell-on clauses as development income.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph-last">
        UEFA's Financial Fair Play rules (now called PSR: Profitability and Sustainability Rules) attempt to prevent clubs spending beyond their means. Clubs must not lose more than €30m per three-year cycle (with exceptions for infrastructure and youth). Manchester City were charged with 115 alleged Premier League rule breaches in 2023; Everton and Nottingham Forest received points deductions in the same season.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Ownership models -->
    <div class="card-football card">
      <div class="clabel">Ownership models: fans, billionaires, states, and the Super League</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Football club ownership has diversified radically since the 1990s. The Bundesliga's <em>50+1 rule</em> requires clubs to retain majority voting rights with their members: no single external investor can hold more than 49% of voting shares. Bayer Leverkusen and Wolfsburg (owned by Bayer and VW respectively, as founders) are exceptions. The rule has prevented the kind of debt-loading seen in English football, but critics argue it restricts investment and competitiveness in European competition.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        State-backed ownership is the dominant force at the top. Manchester City (Abu Dhabi United Group), PSG (Qatar Sports Investments), and Newcastle United (Saudi PIF) are effectively sovereign-wealth assets. This creates an asymmetric advantage: losses are absorbed by national wealth funds without commercial consequence, allowing indefinite investment.
      </p>
      <div class="warn-box">
        The European Super League proposal (April 2021) lasted 48 hours. Twelve clubs, including all six Premier League "Big Six", announced a breakaway competition with guaranteed entry. Fan protests, government threats, UEFA/FIFA sanctions, and broadcast pressure caused all but three clubs (Real Madrid, Barcelona, Juventus) to withdraw within two days. The episode revealed that football clubs are not purely commercial entities: their legitimacy depends on a relationship with supporters that cannot be fully overridden by ownership.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Champions League -->
    <div class="card-football card">
      <div class="clabel">The Champions League: the most valuable club competition in sport</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        The UEFA Champions League (rebranded from the European Cup in 1992–93) generates ~€3.5 billion in annual revenue, distributed primarily by performance and coefficient (historical results). Real Madrid have won it a record 14 times; AC Milan and Bayern Munich have 6 each. The 2023–24 final (Real Madrid 2–0 Borussia Dortmund) drew a global audience of 450 million viewers.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        The <em>UEFA coefficient</em> system awards points for performance over five seasons, determining seedings and access. Clubs with high historical coefficients receive guaranteed group stage entry and better prize money even in poor seasons, creating a self-reinforcing hierarchy. From 2024–25, a new 36-team league-phase format replaced the group stage, giving the largest clubs two additional guaranteed matches per season, worth roughly €20m each.
      </p>
      <div class="clabel u-mt-md">Champions League prize money breakdown (2023–24 approximate)</div>
      <div class="u-mt-sm" id="ucl-chart"></div>
      <p class="data-note">Figures in millions EUR. Market pool distributions vary by broadcaster market size. Source: UEFA 2024.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- ── PART H ── -->
    <div class="sec-football">Part H · the major leagues: how the world's top competitions work</div>

    <div class="card-football card">
      <div class="clabel">The Premier League: the world's most watched domestic league</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        The Premier League was founded in 1992 when the First Division clubs broke from the Football League to negotiate their own TV deal, principally with BSkyB (now Sky). That first deal was £304 million for five years. The 2022–25 UK rights deal was worth £5.1 billion for domestic rights alone; adding international rights (sold to 189 countries) takes total broadcast revenue above £10 billion per cycle. The league is broadcast in more countries than any other sports competition.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Revenue is distributed unequally but more evenly than most leagues: the "Big Six" (Arsenal, Chelsea, Liverpool, Man City, Man United, Tottenham) receive more from commercial and European competition, but equal share broadcasting ensures even promoted clubs earn ~£110m per season. This financial cushion is one reason English clubs perform well in Europe.
      </p>
      <div class="info-box">
        Promotion and relegation is the key structural difference from American sports. Three clubs are promoted from the Championship each year; three Premier League clubs are relegated. The financial gap between the two divisions is roughly £170m per season, creating intense pressure around the relegation zone. "Parachute payments" (three years of reduced Premier League distributions to relegated clubs) partially cushion the drop, but also arguably impede competition within the Championship.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card-football card">
      <div class="clabel">La Liga, Bundesliga, Serie A, Ligue 1: how they differ</div>

      <div class="clabel u-mb-sm">European league comparison</div>
      <div class="explorer-btns" id="league-btns"></div>
      <div class="explorer-panel" id="league-panel">
        <div class="ep-title">Select a league to explore</div>
        <div class="ep-body">Each of the five major European leagues has a distinct financial structure, ownership culture, and competitive balance profile.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card-football card">
      <div class="clabel">South American football: CONMEBOL, Libertadores, and the culture</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        The Copa Libertadores, South America's Champions League, is contested by clubs from CONMEBOL's 10 member nations. It is widely considered the most intense club competition in football: Boca Juniors vs River Plate in the 2018 final was broadcast in 80 countries and decided across two legs with a cumulative 5–3 scoreline. The physical and emotional intensity of CONMEBOL football is higher than European competition, partly because South American fans have a more direct and confrontational relationship with players.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        The <em>Superclásico</em>, Boca Juniors vs River Plate, is a contest between two Buenos Aires clubs that represent class and geography: Boca is the working-class port district; River is the wealthier Núñez neighbourhood. They share a city but have never shared mutual warmth. The rivalry is not simply sporting; it is woven into Argentine identity and has at times suspended normal civic relations.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph-last">
        The financial drain of talent to Europe is existential. Brazil's domestic league, the Brasileirao, loses its best players annually: Vinicius Jr left Flamengo for Real Madrid at 17, before playing a single top-division game. Argentina's Primera División has seen its top 10 earners earn less collectively than the Premier League minimum salary. The talent pipeline flows one direction.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card-football card">
      <div class="clabel">African, Asian, MLS, and the growing competitions</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        The <em>Africa Cup of Nations (AFCON)</em>, held biennially by CAF, is one of the most attended continental tournaments in the world. Senegal won their first title in 2022; Egypt holds the record with seven wins. The tournament has been criticised for clashing with European club fixtures, as roughly 80% of Africa's top players now earn their living in Europe.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        The <em>Saudi Pro League's</em> 2023 investment wave (signing Ronaldo, Benzema, Neymar, and over 20 other high-profile players on contracts above €80m per year) represents either a new Gulf state model (similar to PSG's origins) or a temporary vanity project. Saudi clubs are backed by PIF funds; the league generated significant global media coverage but has not yet achieved competitive legitimacy comparable to the top five European leagues.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph-last">
        <em>MLS</em>, Major League Soccer, has grown from 10 founding clubs in 1996 to 29 in 2023, with expansion clubs costing $300–400m in franchise fees. Lionel Messi's arrival at Inter Miami in 2023 drove a 1,000% increase in MLS streaming subscriptions in a single week. The league uses a designated player rule (the "Beckham Rule," introduced for David Beckham's 2007 arrival at LA Galaxy) to allow each club to sign up to three players outside salary cap constraints.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- ── PART I ── -->
    <div class="sec-football">Part I · football and culture: identity, tribalism, and meaning</div>

    <div class="card-football card">
      <div class="clabel">Football and identity: why fans care so much</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Henri Tajfel's social identity theory holds that humans define themselves partly through group membership, and that the group's status affects individual self-esteem. Sport exploits this mechanism: fan loyalty is largely involuntary, inherited from family, geography, and class. Research by Robert Cialdini coined "BIRGing" (Basking In Reflected Glory): fans are more likely to wear team colours after a win, and "CORFing" (Cutting Off Reflected Failure), distancing themselves after defeat, shifting language from "we won" to "they lost."
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Football clubs often perform functions previously fulfilled by religion or political parties: regular communal gatherings, shared rituals, a narrative of struggle and transcendence, and a community of strangers bound by abstract loyalty. Nick Hornby's <em>Fever Pitch</em> (1992) is the canonical literary document of this relationship; its insight, that football provides an emotional intensity otherwise unavailable to modern men, remains accurate and slightly sad.
      </p>
      <div class="info-box">
        National team support is stronger than club support in most countries outside England and Germany. In Argentina, England is not "just another country": the 1966 World Cup quarterfinal, the Malvinas war, and the Hand of God goal constitute a 30-year narrative thread. National football rivalries encode historical grievances that diplomacy cannot fully address.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card-football card">
      <div class="clabel">Ultras, hooliganism, and fan culture</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Italian <em>ultras</em> emerged in the late 1960s: organized supporter groups occupying designated curvas (ends), producing choreographed tifo displays, and claiming an ownership stake in the club's identity distinct from commercial spectacle. The best ultras culture creates genuinely spectacular atmospheres: the Borussia Dortmund Yellow Wall (Südtribüne), Europe's largest standing terrace at 25,000 capacity, is the most replicated supporter model globally. The worst ultras culture involves violence, racism, political extremism, and criminal extortion of clubs.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        English football hooliganism peaked between 1975 and 1990. The Heysel disaster (1985), 39 Juventus fans killed before the European Cup final in Brussels, led to English clubs being banned from European competition for five years. The Hillsborough disaster (1989), 97 Liverpool fans killed in a crush at Sheffield Wednesday's ground, was initially blamed on fan behaviour; the truth (police failure) took 23 years to be officially established.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph-last">
        Fan protests in the 2020s have targeted ownership. Manchester United fans stormed Old Trafford in May 2021 to protest against the Glazer family's leveraged buyout (which saddled the club with £500m in debt). The Leeds United fan protests against "dirty Leeds" imagery in the 1970s and the "Green and Gold" campaign (Manchester United scarves in Newton Heath's original colours, 2010) show that fans retain genuine power when they act collectively.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card-football card">
      <div class="clabel">Football and race: discrimination, progress, and what still needs to change</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Racist abuse in European stadia has been documented since at least the 1970s. Monkey chants, banana throwing, and coordinated abuse from home and away fans became so normalised in Italy and Spain that it was treated as background noise. AC Milan's George Weah (1995 Ballon d'Or winner) faced weekly racial abuse; in 2023, Italy midfielder Mike Maignan walked off the pitch at Udine to protest racial abuse, prompting a match suspension.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Kick It Out, founded in England in 1993, monitors and reports discrimination in football. The structural issue it highlights: while approximately 43% of Premier League players are Black, fewer than 5% of head coaches and 3% of boardroom positions are held by Black or minority ethnic individuals. The Rooney Rule (requiring at least one BAME candidate to be interviewed for senior roles) was adopted by the FA in 2018 but has produced limited change.
      </p>
      <div class="warn-box">
        The 2021 Euros final penalty shootout (Italy beat England) was followed by immediate racist abuse targeting Rashford, Sancho, and Saka, all of whom had missed penalties. A YouGov poll found 57% of England fans believed the abuse was racist; a further poll found 80% of Black Britons had experienced online racial abuse in their lifetimes. The abuse was swift, organised, and indisputably racial, not random anger at missed penalties.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card-football card">
      <div class="clabel">Women's football: the fastest growing sport in the world</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        The 2023 Women's World Cup in Australia and New Zealand broke every attendance and viewership record: the final (Spain 1–0 England) drew 32 million viewers in the UK alone, more than the men's team's equivalent in 2022. Total tournament attendance of 1.98 million exceeded the previous record by 40%.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        The Women's Super League (England) has seen average attendance treble since 2019, with Arsenal and Chelsea regularly filling 25,000-seat venues. The FA Women's Super League broadcast deal with Sky and BBC (2022–25) is worth £24m, still a fraction of the men's game, but growing. Spain's Liga F, Germany's Frauen Bundesliga, and the NWSL (USA) are the other major leagues.
      </p>
      <div class="clabel u-mt-md">Women's World Cup final viewership growth</div>
      <div class="u-mt-sm" id="wwc-chart"></div>
      <p class="data-note">UK television viewers, millions. Sources: BARB, BBC Sport.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- ── PART J ── -->
    <div class="sec-football">Part J · coaching and management: leading a football club</div>

    <div class="card-football card">
      <div class="clabel">What a manager actually does, and how the role has changed</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        The 19th-century football manager was primarily a secretary, organising fixtures, travel, and facilities. Tactical authority remained with players (often the team captain). The shift to manager-as-tactician happened gradually between 1900–1950; Herbert Chapman at Arsenal (1925–1934) is the first clearly modern manager, redesigning Chapman's position on the pitch, introducing the WM formation, installing floodlights, and even persuading London Underground to rename Gillespie Road station "Arsenal."
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        The modern manager operates within a <em>sporting director model</em>: a continental structure where recruitment, contracts, and squad planning are handled by a sporting director, leaving the manager free to focus on coaching and tactics. Liverpool's Julian Ward (later Michael Edwards) and Brighton's Paul Barber exemplify this. The alternative is the traditional English "manager-in-charge-of-everything" model, which Ferguson at Manchester United practised until 2013 but which is increasingly rare.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph-last">
        Backroom staff has exploded since 2000. A Premier League club's technical team now routinely includes: assistant manager, first team coach, goalkeeping coach, set-piece specialist, sports scientists (2–3), nutritionists (1–2), video analysts (2–4), performance analysts (2–3), data scientists (1–3), physiotherapists (3–5), sports psychologist, and a head of recruitment with a team of scouts. A 1990s top-flight club employed perhaps 8 people in coaching and support; today it is 30–50.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Great managers explorer -->
    <div class="card-football card">
      <div class="clabel">The great managers: a comparative explorer</div>
      <div class="explorer-btns" id="manager-btns"></div>
      <div class="explorer-panel" id="manager-panel">
        <div class="ep-title">Select a manager above</div>
        <div class="ep-body">Each of these figures transformed not just their clubs but the way the game is understood and played. Click to explore.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Managerial turnover -->
    <div class="card-football card">
      <div class="clabel">Managerial turnover: the research on sacking</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        The average Premier League manager tenure is approximately 1.4 years (2015–2023 data). The intuition behind sacking, that a new manager will improve results, is supported in the short term but collapses over longer time frames. A 2007 study by Bas ter Weel found that while clubs showed improved results in the first 8 games after a new appointment, this improvement was indistinguishable from statistical regression to the mean: the team was sacked at a performance trough and would have improved regardless.
      </p>
      <div class="clabel u-mt-md">Factors that actually predict managerial success (research-based)</div>
      <div class="u-mt-sm" id="mgr-success-chart"></div>
      <p class="data-note">Relative importance based on econometric studies (Szymanski, ter Weel, Bridgewater). Wage bill is the dominant predictor of league performance regardless of manager.</p>

      <div class="clabel gap-above-lg">Time-to-results simulator</div>
