        <input type="range" id="mgr-tenure" min="0" max="48" value="6" step="1">
        <div class="slider-out" id="mgr-tenure-out">Move the slider to explore how manager tenure relates to performance.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── PART K ── -->
    <h2 class="sec" id="part-k-other-major-sports-a-working-knowledge">Part K · other major sports: a working knowledge</h2>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="clabel">Basketball: the NBA, global reach, and how the game works</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Basketball was invented by James Naismith in December 1891 in Springfield, Massachusetts, with a soccer ball and two peach baskets nailed to a gymnasium balcony. The first formal rules allowed nine players per side and prohibited running with the ball. The NBA was founded in 1946 (as the BAA) and merged with the rival NBL in 1949. Today it has 30 franchises valued at an average of $3.85 billion (Forbes 2023), with the Golden State Warriors valued at $7.7 billion.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        The <em>three-point revolution</em> is the most profound tactical shift in basketball history. In the 2012–13 season, NBA teams took an average of 18.4 three-point attempts per game; by 2022–23 that had risen to 35.1. The analytics driver was simple: 33% accuracy from three-point range produces 1.0 expected points per shot, equal to 50% accuracy on a two-pointer. Teams with elite three-point shooters (Golden State Warriors 2015–2019) unlocked an efficiency advantage that forced opponents to adapt or lose.
      </p>
      <div class="info-box">
        The GOAT debate: Michael Jordan's Chicago Bulls won 6 championships in 8 seasons (1991–98) without ever losing an NBA Final. LeBron James has played more NBA minutes, reached more Finals, and statistically dominated every era he competed in, but his Finals record is 4–6. Jordan's defenders argue he never lost a Finals series; James' defenders note he dragged inferior teams to Finals Jordan could not have won. The debate is structurally unresolvable because the comparison requires counterfactual reasoning.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="clabel">Tennis: Grand Slams, surfaces, and the scoring system</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Tennis has four Grand Slams: Australian Open (Melbourne, January, hard court), Roland Garros (Paris, May–June, clay), Wimbledon (London, June–July, grass), and the US Open (New York, August–September, hard court). Surface differences are not aesthetic: clay slows the ball 20–30% and increases bounce height significantly, favouring baseliners; grass is fast and low, favouring serve-and-volley; hard courts split the difference.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        The scoring system: 15, 30, 40, game; games, sets, match, is historically derived from medieval French real tennis and has little logical basis. The tiebreak (played at 6–6 in a set) was introduced by Jimmy Van Alen in the 1960s to prevent marathon sets. Wimbledon resisted it until 1971. The "super-tiebreak" final set (played to 10 points) replaced full final sets at all Slams except Wimbledon by 2022.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph-last">
        The "Big Three" era (Federer, Nadal, Djokovic, 2003–2023) produced 65 of the 83 possible Grand Slam titles between them. Djokovic holds the all-time record with 24 Grand Slams. The structural reason for their dominance: all three peaked simultaneously, reducing opportunities for any single rival to establish consistency. Carlos Alcaraz (born 2003) appears the most likely successor.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="clabel">Cricket: the longest and most complicated game</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Cricket exists in three formats of radically different character. <em>Test cricket</em> is played over five days, two innings each side, with no result being a legitimate outcome. It is the oldest format (first Test, England vs Australia, 1877) and considered the deepest test of skill, character, and concentration. <em>ODIs</em> (One Day Internationals, 50 overs per side) were introduced in 1971 and became the World Cup format. <em>T20</em> (Twenty20, 20 overs per side, ~3.5 hours) was introduced in 2003 and has become the dominant commercial format: the IPL (Indian Premier League), founded in 2008, now generates ~$11 billion in brand value (BCCI estimate 2023).
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        The LBW (Leg Before Wicket) rule is cricket's offside, frequently misunderstood. A batsman is out LBW if the ball hits their pad (without first hitting the bat) and would have gone on to hit the stumps. The DRS (Decision Review System) uses ball-tracking technology (Hawk-Eye) to project the ball's trajectory, but the projection carries an uncertainty range, and umpire's-call decisions (where the ball clips the edge of the stumps) remain subjective.
      </p>
      <div class="warn-box">
        The T20 format has fundamentally changed batting technique. Reverse sweeps, ramps, scoops, and switch-hits that were considered trick shots in 2000 are now standard deliveries for international batsmen. Bowling averages in T20 have declined across all categories as batsmen optimise for aggressive scoring at every stage. This affects Test cricket: modern Test batsmen are more aggressive than previous generations and Test match run rates have risen steadily since 2005.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="clabel">American football, rugby, and the other football codes</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        American football diverged from association football via rugby: Harvard, Yale, and Princeton adopted rugby-style rules in the 1870s; Walter Camp's rules changes (1880s) (the line of scrimmage, the down-and-distance system) created distinctly American football. The NFL, founded in 1920, is now the most lucrative sports league in the world, generating $18.6 billion in revenue in 2022.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Rugby union (15-a-side) and rugby league (13-a-side) split in 1895 over the same amateur/professional divide as football. Rugby union retained amateur status until 1995; professionalism transformed the game: previously undersize clubs now fell behind those that could pay. The World Cup final (2023: South Africa 12–11 New Zealand) is the sport's pinnacle. The Super Bowl is the single most-watched US television event each year: the 2023 broadcast drew 113 million viewers.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="clabel">Combat sports: boxing, MMA, and the tradition of fighting</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Boxing was the first combat sport to be fully codified in the modern era (Queensberry Rules, 1867). Today's professional boxing has 17 weight classes (strawweight to heavyweight) and four major sanctioning bodies (WBC, WBA, IBF, WBO), each of which has a "world champion", creating a confusing proliferation of titles. The undisputed heavyweight championship (holding all four belts simultaneously) was unified only twice between 1990 and 2021: Lennox Lewis (1999) and Oleksandr Usyk (2021).
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Mixed martial arts (MMA) and the UFC were founded in 1993 with the explicit purpose of testing which martial art was most effective. Early tournaments were won by Royce Gracie using Brazilian jiu-jitsu: ground submission techniques. Modern elite MMA fighters are highly cross-trained: the competitive advantage has shifted from a single specialization to the ability to impose one's preferred fighting range while denying the opponent theirs.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="clabel">Formula 1: technology, money, and speed</div>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        Formula 1's commercial value rests on a unique combination: the world's most expensive sporting technology, a global calendar (23+ races across 5 continents), and a narratively compelling driver/team dynamic. The FIA sets car regulations that define the competitive field; teams spend between $140 million (cost cap, introduced 2021) and ~$400 million total per year on car development, staff, and logistics.
      </p>
      <p class="body-paragraph">
        The <em>constructor vs driver championship</em> distinction is important: Ferrari has won the most drivers' championships (15) across history, but Mercedes won 8 consecutive constructors' championships (2014–2021) in the V6 hybrid era: arguably the most dominant era in team performance in F1 history. Pit stop strategy involves real-time decisions about tyre compounds (soft, medium, hard, with different life and pace profiles) that can negate a 20-second on-track deficit in a single stop.
      </p>
      <div class="success-box">
        Netflix's "Drive to Survive" (launched 2019) is the clearest documented case of a sports documentary expanding a sport's global audience. F1's US fanbase grew from ~1 million fans in 2016 to an estimated 6 million in 2022, directly correlating with the show's three seasons. Las Vegas was added to the calendar in 2023 with a reported $375 million city investment: a market that barely existed for F1 before Netflix.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── PART L ── -->
    <h2 class="sec" id="part-l-q-and-a">Part L · Q&A</h2>

    <div class="qa">
      <div class="qa-q">Is sport inherently meritocratic, or does it just look that way?</div>
      <button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button>
      <div class="qa-ans">Sport is significantly less meritocratic than it appears. Relative age effect, the advantage of being born early in a selection cohort year, means January-born players are dramatically overrepresented in elite football academies (Malcolm Gladwell popularized this in Outliers; the original research is by Roger Barnsley, 1992). Socioeconomic access shapes who reaches elite sport: swimming, tennis, and golf have enormous upfront cost barriers; even football requires consistent nutrition, recovery time, and access to coaching that disadvantaged families cannot provide. Race compounds geography: in England, Black players dominate the pitch but are absent from management boards, suggesting talent alone does not determine career ceiling. The meritocracy narrative serves both sport and ideology: it makes inequality look like natural selection.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa">
      <div class="qa-q">Does VAR improve the fairness of football, or does it just move the argument?</div>
      <button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button>
      <div class="qa-ans">VAR reduces clear errors: the 2022 World Cup had zero incorrect red card decisions reach a final ruling, compared to roughly 3–4 in previous tournaments. But it has created new categories of controversy: marginal offside decisions (a 2cm armpit), "clear and obvious error" subjectivity, and inconsistency between leagues in their application threshold. A 2021 YouGov poll found 55% of Premier League fans believed VAR had made the sport worse. The core problem is philosophical: football's social contract involves shared uncertainty: moments of disputed, contested, argued meaning. Replacing that uncertainty with (imperfect) algorithmic precision changes what kind of thing football is.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa">
      <div class="qa-q">Can analytics fully replace traditional scouting in identifying talent?</div>
      <button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button>
      <div class="qa-ans">No, and the best practitioners say so. Data models excel at identifying patterns in large samples and correcting for scout bias (halo effects, aesthetic preferences). They fail at qualitative factors that barely appear in data: a player's response to adversity, leadership within a group, injury risk from biomechanical movement, or tactical intelligence. Brighton's Paul Barber has argued that their analytical approach is to narrow the scouting pool to players statistically undervalued by the market, then apply human judgement to select within that pool. The model reduces error rate and search cost; it does not replace the scout's contextual judgement at the decisive step.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa">
      <div class="qa-q">Why don't more countries have competitive national football teams despite producing enormous populations of players?</div>
      <button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button>
      <div class="qa-ans">Population alone is insufficient. India (1.4 billion people) ranks around 100th in FIFA rankings. The key variables are: investment in youth development infrastructure, coaching quality at grassroots level, competitive domestic leagues that develop players before international level, and cultural embedding of football (India's dominant sport is cricket). Brazil's success is partly population, but mainly its dense national football culture: every favela has a futsal court; the game is played and watched at every level of society. Nigeria and Ghana consistently produce elite players despite per-capita GDP far below European nations, because urban football culture is intense. China has a population of 1.4 billion and has invested billions in football development since 2015 but still ranked 79th in 2023.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa">
      <div class="qa-q">Is the pay gap in women's sport a market outcome or a structural injustice?</div>
      <button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button>
      <div class="qa-ans">It is both, and the two are connected. Market outcomes in sport are downstream of historical investment, scheduling, and broadcast decisions, and women's football received essentially zero broadcast investment before 2010. The BBC showing the 2019 Women's World Cup in primetime increased women's FA Cup final viewership from 350,000 to 4.1 million overnight. Norway's national football team reached equal pay parity with the men's team in 2017; the US Women's National Team settled equal pay litigation with US Soccer for $24 million in 2022. The argument that "women's sport earns less, so should pay less" ignores that earnings in sport are determined by prior investment choices, not inherent audience interest. The 2023 Women's World Cup final viewership proved there is mass appetite when broadcast infrastructure supports it.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa">
      <div class="qa-q">Does home advantage in football have a known cause?</div>
      <button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button>
      <div class="qa-ans">Home advantage is real and consistent: in the Premier League, home teams win approximately 46% of matches versus 28% for away teams (draw rate ~26%). Three causal mechanisms are supported by evidence. First, crowd noise increases referee bias toward the home team: studies of Serie A during the COVID-19 empty-stadium period (2020) showed home advantage nearly disappeared, reducing home wins to 38.7% vs 45.3% in comparable preceding seasons. Second, travel fatigue disadvantages the away team, particularly for fixture congestion. Third, familiarity with the pitch (size, surface quality, goalpost thickness) provides genuine tactical advantage at home. The crowd noise mechanism accounts for roughly 40–50% of home advantage.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa">
      <div class="qa-q">Is the 100-metre sprint world record bound to continue falling, or have we reached the human limit?</div>
      <button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button>
      <div class="qa-ans">Biomechanical modelling by Mark Denny (2008, Journal of Experimental Biology) and updated by John Barrow suggest an absolute physiological limit around 9.48 seconds, meaning Usain Bolt's 9.58 (2009) is 0.10s from the wall. But the same models set the limit for women at 10.19s, and the current world record (Florence Griffith-Joyner, 1988) is 10.49, suggesting the women's side is underperforming relative to the model, likely due to the doping era distortions. Improvements in sprint performance now come primarily from spikes technology (Nike's Alphafly contributed ~1% efficiency gains), altitude training, and track surface. The gap between current records and the physiological ceiling is measured in hundredths of a second.</div>
    </div>

  

  
  
