      </div>`;
    }).join('');
    const axisMarks = [1863, 1880, 1900, 1920, 1940, 1960, 1980, 2000, 2024];
    html += `<div class="pc-axis">${axisMarks.map(y => `<span class="micro-caption">${y}</span>`).join('')}</div>`;
    ttEl.innerHTML = html;

    // ─── Formation explorer ───
    const formations = {
      '4-3-3': {
        title: '4-3-3',
        body: 'Four defenders, three central midfielders, and three forwards. The dominant European formation since 2008. Guardiola\'s Barcelona used it as a pressing and possession system; Klopp\'s Liverpool as a gegenpressing structure. The three midfielders can split into different shapes (6-8-10 vertically; or a flat three). Weakness: central midfield can be outnumbered if the opponent plays a 4-5-1 or double pivot.'
      },
      '4-2-3-1': {
        title: '4-2-3-1',
        body: 'A double pivot (two defensive midfielders) shields the back four, a playmaking number 10 operates behind a single striker, with wide midfielders providing width. José Mourinho\'s Chelsea and Inter used this to devastating effect. The double pivot creates defensive solidity and allows full-backs to advance, but the single striker must work intensely to hold the ball alone.'
      },
      '4-4-2': {
        title: '4-4-2 (flat)',
        body: 'The classic English formation: two banks of four, two strikers. Dominant in England from 1960 to 2005. The partnership between the two strikers creates unique combination play (one dropping, one running beyond). Alex Ferguson used it at Manchester United until Martin Keown and Thierry Henry exposed it with Arsenal\'s 4-2-3-1. Now rarely used at elite level because it leaves central midfield numerically vulnerable.'
      },
      '3-5-2': {
        title: '3-5-2 / 3-4-3',
        body: 'Three centre-backs with two wing-backs who act as the wide component of both defence and attack. Requires physically exceptional wing-backs who can cover the full flank. Antonio Conte used it at Juventus, Chelsea, and Inter Milan, winning league titles at all three. The system\'s vulnerability: if a wing-back is caught high and the ball is switched, the back three face a 2-vs-2 with no cover. Requires enormous fitness from the wing-backs.'
